  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry weather, slightly below average \ntemperatures, and lower humidity to end the week and start the \nweekend.\n\nAn upper level trough lingers over New England and Atlantic \nCanada this afternoon, with another trough over the upper \nMidwest. This places the Mid-Atlantic region in dry NW flow on \nthe leading flank of an upper ridge. At the surface, weak high \npressure is centered over the region. Quite pleasant for the end\nof July with temperatures in the mid/upper 80s and dewpoints in\nthe upper 50s to mid 60s. Partly sunny with SCT afternoon CU. \nHumidity will likely come up a bit this evening, especially near\nthe coast as the flow becomes onshore. One last dry day (but \nwarmer/more humid) on Saturday as upper heights build a bit as \nthe ridge and surface high nudge offshore and the LLVL flow \nbecomes southerly. Highs near 90F w/ aftn dew pts rising into \nthe mid-upper 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A typical summertime pattern returns by late \nthis weekend and early next week with increasing shower/storm \nchances starting Sunday and continuing through early next week.\n\nThe pattern becomes more unsettled late in the weekend into \nearly next week as a slow moving UL trough approaches from the \nwest. At the same time, a weak cold front approaches from the \nnorth, but will remain north of the local area through Monday \n(and likely wash out by Tue). With the approaching trough and \nBermuda high pressure well offshore, deep-layered S to SW flow \nwill prevail. This will allow rich Gulf moisture to overspread \nthe area on Sunday and remain over us early next week. PWs surge\nfrom a comfortable 1-1.4\" on Saturday to 2.2-2.4\" by late \nSunday/Sunday night. Scattered to potentially numerous \nshowers/tstms are expected Sunday. The highest precip chances \nwill initially be across south-central VA during the aftn (as \nthe deep moisture arrives here first). Showers and tstms should \ngradually spread NE through the evening (though it may very well\nremain dry near the immediate coast and on the eastern shore). \nShowers/tstms are likely across the entire area on Monday as a \nlead shortwave potentially tracks over the area. Monday appears \nto be the day with the highest convective coverage during the \nperiod. The trough dampens a bit from Tuesday/Wednesday, but \nscattered showers and tstms are still expected during the \naftn/evening on both days as weak SW flow aloft continues and \ndeep moisture remains nearby. Given the environment and \nrelatively weak unidirectional flow aloft and tall/skinny CAPE, \nthere will be a threat of localized flash flooding each day. \nTemps dip down a little below average during the early week \nperiod thanks to the cloud cover and multiple rounds of rain.",
